<p><strong>FM-8: v6 selector's monotone <span class="mono">keptEver</span> + guards → asymptotically never evicts</strong> — telemetry: "all N branches (within k)" on every turn of every iter7 replicate. Early runs keep everything (N ≤ k), locking those in via monotone membership forever; late runs keep everything because guards (open questions / failing tests / solver annotations) exempt most of the remaining branches. The two protections interact: properties *added to make eviction safer* make eviction *never happen*.</p>
<p><strong>What tier-2 "DSA is inert" finding buys:</strong> the selector was not simply untuned (fixable with data + more iterations) — it was proving inert *as a reflex*, the direct consequence of stacking safety guards without a release valve. Asking "can I evict this" on every turn in a cache-prefix system is structurally hard: eviction is destructive (branch detail truly disappears from the prompt), so each guard is a unilateral veto. The cumulative effect of multiple veto axes (membership, open questions, test failures, cost heuristics) is that *every* branch qualifies for protection at some turn, and once locked (keptEver), never unlocks — that's not a selector bug, it's a selector ceiling.</p>

