The comment in `packages/core/src/assemble/assembler.ts` (seq 590) says:

```typescript
// The descendant map goes LAST: it grows with every edit, and churn ahead
// of the append-only event stream voids the events' cache (see
// renderActiveMap). Callers that cache Zone C must keep it after their
// moving breakpoint.
```

That comment documents the physical requirement: if you're implementing provider caching on Zone C (as v5.5/v5.6 does via `toZoneCCachedRequest` in `eval/src/loop.ts`), the `C:map` block must *never* have a cache breakpoint on it — it has to ride *after* the last-cached event block's breakpoint. If the map has a breakpoint, or if you try to cache it, you've just made the whole Zone C prefix unstable: file edits expand the map, churn voids the cached bytes ahead of it, and the append-only event stream you meant to cache cheaply re-bills at 1× every turn (that's FM-7). The rule is: **stable content cached, churny content uncached.**

The analogous comment in `renderActiveMap` (seq 588) explains why the map exists separately from the header at all — it's the removal of the volatile seqRanges that were there before (in the old `renderActiveHeader` signature).

